    "reading": {
        "body": "Across 55 tracked repositories, cumulative star counts rose by roughly 31,000 in the eighteen days ending September 1, 2026, an increase of about 1,700 stars per day, with no sign of the rate slowing.\n\nThe fifteen most-starred repositories span the full stack from training frameworks to local inference tools to agent orchestration layers. tensorflow/tensorflow and pytorch/pytorch represent the compute-adjacent foundation, where changes propagate into everything above them. ollama/ollama, ggml-org/llama.cpp, and vllm-project/vllm are each oriented toward running models more efficiently on available hardware, which reflects sustained developer interest in squeezing more inference out of existing resources. The agent and tooling layer, represented by AutoGPT, langchain, cline, and others, sits on top of that inference capacity and depends on it entirely.\n\nThe daily series records total stars, not commits or contributor counts, so it measures attention and perceived relevance rather than engineering output directly. The data does not distinguish between a repository gaining stars because its software improved and one gaining stars because a viral post drove traffic. The pushed_7d and pushed_30d figures, 47 and 49 respectively, indicate that nearly all 55 tracked repositories received commits in the past month, meaning the activity is broad rather than concentrated in a handful of projects. What the data cannot say is whether that breadth reflects parallel progress or duplicated effort across competing approaches to the same problems.",
